-240" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320888/field-1/557015e94af5fa4bd02ae0ff131d8572cb165f5648829317508c9b9e01215bfe" :answer/id #uuid "0defb882-5142-5422-aa53-5788500c0972" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "240" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320888/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf363c20-7a12-5225-abb8-7160a28e988e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-320888/field-1/5842e46cedf2d86768dbd9e6d6d2a317313472d795798010656ec3a0faf5071b" :answer-field/choices ["q-320888/field-1/5842e46cedf2d86768dbd9e6d6d2a317313472d795798010656ec3a0faf5071b" "q-320888/field-1/557015e94af5fa4bd02ae0ff131d8572cb165f5648829317508c9b9e01215bfe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-320888" :question/id #uuid "92ff9de5-848d-514f-9cce-7a8114e6d927" :question/math-academy-id "q-320888" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({300}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${300}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${300}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =4\\cdot (-{60}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={-240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-320888/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-320888"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-24770"] :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-321100/field-1/1dee3a8b4e2e69484264522b7c3f6207b09ecde7ad469649dd240ad6a8e6346d" :answer/id #uuid "6b25d3f3-a8df-57ad-b278-9ef793b8ed2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321100/field-1/608b697f811192589eec3729e28e5d056be7cd5127af1a846d717a33508ae203" :answer/id #uuid "3331941a-dacd-5ae1-aaa3-380deb86c652" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321100/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c903b6ff-51f9-5ce4-99c3-cde7cc748304" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321100/field-1/1dee3a8b4e2e694842645